2 CONT
2 CONT Maria Theresa of Spain
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria Theresa of Spain (French: Marie Thérèse) (September 10, 1638 – July 30, 1683) was the queen consort of France as wife of Louis XIV of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT In France, she was styled, Marie-Thérèse d'Autriche. Her paternal aunt, Anne of Austria (1601-1666), another Spanish princess, also used the Austrian archducal title, then still affected by the Spanish Habsburgs.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early Life
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria Theresa was born at the Escorial as the daughter of Philip IV of Spain and Elisabeth of Bourbon (1602 - 1644).
2 CONT
2 CONT Her paternal grandparents were Philip III of Spain and Margarita of Austria. Her maternal grandparents were Henry IV of France and Marie de' Medici.
2 CONT
2 CONT Philip III was a son of Philip II of Spain and Anna of Austria (1549 - 1580). Anna was a daughter of Maximilian II, Holy Roman Emperor and Maria of Spain.
2 CONT
2 CONT Philip II and Maria of Spain were siblings. They were both children of Charles V, Holy Roman Emperor and Isabella of Portugal. Maria Theresa, therefore, like many Habsburgs, was the product of many years of royal intermarriages be
2 CONC tween cousins.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1659, Jules Cardinal Mazarin, while pretending to seek a bride for his master in Margaret of Savoy, instead induced the king of Spain to make proposals for the marriage of his daughter with Louis XIV. It was an exciting revelat
2 CONC ion for Anne of Austria, mother of Louis XIV, who wished to see her son matched among her Habsburg relatives. [1].
2 CONT
2 CONT Unfortunately, Maria Theresa's agreed dowry was far too large for an impoverished Spain to pay. Instead, the future French Queen had to renounce any claim she or her future children would have to the Spanish succession. Mazarin al
2 CONC so used the agreement to evade certain terms of the marriage contract. Maria Theresa was married on June 9, 1660. Her father, Philip IV, and his entire court accompanied the bride to the Isle of Pheasants in the Bidassoa, where Lo
2 CONC uis met her.
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria Theresa and Louis were double first cousins. His father was Louis XIII of France who was a sibling of her mother Elisabeth of Bourbon. His mother was Anne of Austria who was a sibling of her father Philip IV of Spain.
2 CONT
2 CONT In France
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria Theresa was short, dwarflike and had the Hapsburg lip; the unfortunate product of generations of inbreeding. While she did not suffer the insanity or physical handicaps of her other inbred relatives, her personality had a ch
2 CONC ildlike simplicity to it. [2]. She never learned to speak the French language very well, and her Spanish accent was considered irritating by those at court. Maria Theresa's days were often spent praying with her mother-in-law and 
2 CONC playing cards, as she had no interest in politics or literature.
2 CONT
2 CONT The King was faithful to his wife for the first year of their marriage, but the new queen's amiability and her undoubted virtues failed to secure her husband's regard and affection. Maria Theresa was always the last to know that h
2 CONC er husband had found a new mistress. She saw herself neglected, although, the King always somehow made sure to be in her bed every night. Maria Theresa hated Louise de La Vallière.
2 CONT
2 CONT She tolerated Mme. de Montespan and others, but Marie Thérèse was too pious and too adoring of her husband to openly resent the position in which she was placed by the king's avowed infidelities. Eventually, the Queen acted with d
2 CONC ignity and did not create scenes at court. As a reward the King left her to her own devices, with her dwarves, chocolate and maids. During the period Madame de Maintenon reigned over his mind and affections, the King bestowed more
2 CONC  attention on his wife, which she repaid by lavishing kindness on the mistress.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marie Thérèse only part in political affairs were the years of 1667, 1672, and 1678 when she acted as Regent during Louis XIV's absence on foreign campaigns.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Death
2 CONT
2 CONT She died on 30 July 1683 at Versailles, not without suspicion of foul play on the part of her doctors. There is, however, no real proof that the Queen was poisoned. The modern day belief is that her death was caused by cancer, ste
2 CONC mming from a large tumor under her arm. Her death was probably the only occasion in her life that caused the King any sort of emotion on his part, albeit briefly, apart from his sadness at losing so many legitimate children in inf
2 CONC ancy. Of her six children only one survived her, the dauphin Louis, who died in 1711.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marie Therese's grandson, Philip V of Spain, would eventually come to inherit her succession rights to the Spanish throne, after the death of her mentally unstable half brother Charles II of Spain.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Issue
2 CONT
