2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Élisabeth Charlotte of Orléans
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth Charlotte of Orléans or in its french form Élisabeth Charlotte d'Orléans, styled Mademoiselle de Chartres (Saint-Cloud, September 13, 1676 - Commercy, December 23, 1744), was a French princess and regent of the sovereign
2 CONC  duchy of Lorraine.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early life
2 CONT
2 CONT Élisabeth Charlotte was the only daughter of Philippe de France, duc d'Orléans, (1640-1701), younger brother of King Louis XIV by his second wife, Elizabeth Charlotte, Princess Palatine(1652-1722). Her mother was a daughter of Cha
2 CONC rles I Louis, Elector Palatine. As a granddaughter of Louis XIII of France, she was a petite-fille de France. She was born at the Château de Saint-Cloud outside of Paris on September 13, 1676. She was one of three children. The ot
2 CONC her two were:
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexandre Louis d'Orléans, duc de Valois. (2 June 1673 – 16 March 1676)
2 CONT
2 CONT Died before Élisabeth was born.
2 CONT
2 CONT Philippe II d'Orléans (August 2 1674 – 2 December 1723).
2 CONT
2 CONT He would become the Regent of France during Louis XV's childhood.
2 CONT
2 CONT Male-line ancestor of Philippe Egalité, Louis Philippe I, King of the French, and of the modern Orléanist pretenders to the crown of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Mademoiselle de Chartres
2 CONT
2 CONT As was customary for unmarried young ladies at the French court, she was given an honorary style, Mademoiselle de Chartres, taken from the name of one of her father's appanages. Her half siblings by her father and his first wife, 
2 CONC Princess Henrietta-Anne of England, were:
2 CONT
2 CONT Marie Louise d'Orléans (March 27 1662 – 12 February 1689)
2 CONT
2 CONT Known as Mademoiselle before her marriage to Charles II of Spain.
2 CONT
2 CONT Philippe Charles, duc de Valois (July 16 1664 – 8 December 1666)
2 CONT
2 CONT A daughter born July 9, 1665 ; died after.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne Marie d'Orléans (August 27 1669 – 26 August 1728)
2 CONT
2 CONT Known as Madame Royale before her marriage to Victor Amadeus II of Savoy, first King of Sardinia, in 1684.
2 CONT
2 CONT maternal grandmother of Louis XV of France
2 CONT
2 CONT ancestress of the Kings of Sardinia and Kings of Italy
2 CONT
2 CONT through her derives the post-Stuart legitimist claim of the Jacobites to the English and Scottish thrones.
2 CONT
2 CONT As a child, Élisabeth-Charlotte was described by her mother as 'so terribly wild' and 'rough as a boy'. [1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Her mother hoped to marry her daughter to her first cousin, the widowed Monseigneur or even to his son Louis Duke de Bourgogne, but neither marriage was to be.
2 CONT
2 CONT Possible marriages
2 CONT
2 CONT As her elder half-sisters had all married into prominent reigning families in Europe, she expected the same.[citation needed] When her cousin's wife, Dauphine Victoire said she should marry one of her younger brothers, Élisabeth s
2 CONC aid:
2 CONT
2 CONT I am not made, madame, for a younger son[2][unreliable source?]
2 CONT
2 CONT It was also suggested by some that she should marry her first cousin, Louis-Auguste de Bourbon, duc du Maine, the eldest legitimated son of Louis XIV and his mistress, Madame de Montespan. As her mother despised the king's illegit
2 CONC imate children, the chances of such an alliance were remote. Although spared from the 'horror' of such a mismatch in rank, her brother, the duc de Chartres was not. He married the duc du Maine's younger sister, Françoise-Marie de 
2 CONC Bourbon, in 1692. Élisabeth Charlotte married six years after her elder brother. Before then, her ambitious mother even thought of trying to get her brother-in-law Louis XIV to secure for her daughter a marriage to the King of Eng
2 CONC land, William III, who was rumoured to be homosexual (though this is contested) like her own father.[citation needed]
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and children
2 CONT
2 CONT She married on October 13, 1698 at Fontainebleau Leopold, Duke of Lorraine (1679-1729), son of Charles V, Duke of Lorraine and Eleonora Maria Josefa of Austria.
2 CONT
2 CONT The ducal couple had thirteen children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Léopold (26 Aug 1699 - 2 April 1700)
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth Charlotte (21 Oct 1700 - 4 May 1711)
2 CONT
2 CONT Louise Christine (13 Nov 1701 - 18 Nov 1701)
2 CONT
2 CONT Marie Gabrièle Charlotte (30 Dec 1702 - 11 May 1711)
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis (28 Jan 1704 - 10 May 1711)
2 CONT
2 CONT Josepha Gabrièle (16 Feb 1705 - 25 Mar 1708)
2 CONT
2 CONT Gabrièle Louise (4 Mar 1706 - 13 June 1710)
2 CONT
2 CONT Léopold Clement Charles (25 April 1707 - 4 June 1723)
2 CONT
2 CONT Francis Stephen (8 Dec 1708 - 18 Aug 1765)
2 CONT
2 CONT The heir to the House of Lorraine who later married Empress Maria Theresa of Austria and became Holy Roman Emperor. They were the parents of Queen Marie Antoinette of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Eléonore (4 June 1710 - 28 July 1710)
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth Therese (15 Oct 1711 - 3 July 1741), married in 1737 Charles Emmanuel III of Sardinia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince Charles Alexander of Lorraine (12 Dec 1712 - 4 July 1780), governor of the Austrian Netherlands and army commander.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne Charlotte (1714 - 1773), abbess at Essen.
2 CONT
2 CONT As a surprise to all, what had been expected to be an unhappy union turned out to be marriage of love and happiness.[citation needed] After ten years, however, her husband turned his attentions to another, Anne-Marguerite de Ligné
2 CONC ville, princesse de Beauvau-Craon. Embarrassed, the duchess remained silent and continued to live in the Château de Lunéville, the main residence of the House of Lorraine. After her husband's liaison ended, the couple had five mor
2 CONC e children, one of whom would become the father of Marie Antoinette.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]The Regency to 1737
2 CONT
2 CONT On the death of her uncle Louis XIV in 1715, her elder brother, now the duc d'Orléans, became the Regent of France for the five year old King Louis XV, and the era of his rule is known as the Regency. Louis XV was crowned in Octob
2 CONC er, 1722, two months before the death of her mother in December of the same year. Her brother died in December of the next year. Seven years later, in 1729 her husband died, leaving his wife regent of Lorraine for her son, Françoi
2 CONC s Stephen of Lorraine. After being educated in Vienna, he returned to Lorraine in 1737, ending his mother's tenure as regent.
2 CONT
2 CONT Unable to prevent her son from giving up the duchy of Lorraine (to Stanisław Leszczyński) when he married the Habsburg heiress, Maria Theresa of Austria, she moved into the château d’Harou in nearby Commercy, which was erected int
2 CONC o a sovereign principality for her to enjoy during her dowager years.
2 CONT
2 CONT Later life
2 CONT
2 CONT Élisabeth Charlotte died, at the age of sixty, following a stroke on December 23, 1744. She was the last of her siblings to die and outlived ten of her thirteen children. Nine months after her death, her son François-Stephen becam
2 CONC e the Holy Roman Emperor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Legacy
2 CONT
2 CONT As the mother of the Holy Roman Emperor Francis I, she was an ancestor of the entire 18th and 19th century Habsburg family, as they all descended from Francis I and Maria Theresa. Amongst them are Queen Marie Antoinette and Marie-
2 CONC Louise of Austria, the wife of Napoleon Bonaparte.
2 CONT
