1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ====Golden Fleece - Grand Masters====
2 CONT ====Golden Fleece - Knights: Spanish Branch====
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Links:==
2 CONT *[http://www.thepeerage.com/p10579.htm#i105783 The Peerage]
2 CONT *[http://www.geneall.net/H/per_page.php?id=4956 Geneall]
2 CONT *[http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ferdinand_VI_of_Spain Wikipedia]
2 CONT *'''King of Spain:''' Reign	9 July 1746 – 10 August 1759
2 CONT >'''Predecessor:''' [http://www.geni.com/people/Felipe-V-el-Animoso-rey-de-Espa%C3%B1a/5318340685370041549 Philip V] '''Successor:''' [http://www.geni.com/people/Carlos-III-el-Pol%C3%ADtico-rey-de-Espa%C3%B1a/6000000007279420595 C
2 CONC harles III]
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Ferdinand VI; King of Spain.
2 CONT
2 CONT Reign July 9, 1746–August 10, 1759 
2 CONT
2 CONT Consort Barbara of Portugal 
2 CONT
2 CONT Father Philip V 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother Maria Luisa of Savoy 
2 CONT
2 CONT Born September 23, 1713
2 CONT
2 CONT Madrid, Spain 
2 CONT
2 CONT Died August 10, 1759 (aged 45)
2 CONT
2 CONT Villaviciosa de Odón, Madrid, Spain
2 CONT
2 CONT Ferdinand VI, (September 23, 1713 – August 10, 1759), King of Spain from 1746 until his death, second son of Philip V, founder of the Spanish Bourbon dynasty (as opposed to the French Bourbons), by his first marriage with Maria Lo
2 CONC uisa of Savoy, was born at Madrid on September 23, 1713.
2 CONT
2 CONT His youth was depressed. His father's second wife, Elizabeth Farnese, was a domineering woman, who had no affection except for her own children, and who looked upon her stepson as an obstacle to their fortunes. The hypochondria of
2 CONC  his father left Elizabeth mistress of the palace.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ferdinand was married in 1729 to Maria Barbara of Braganza, daughter of John V of Portugal and Mary Anne of Austria. The very homely looks of his wife were thought by observers to cause the prince a visible shock when he was first
2 CONC  presented to her. Yet he became deeply attached to his wife, and proved in fact nearly as uxorious as his father.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ferdinand was by temperament melancholy, shy and distrustful of his own abilities. When complimented on his shooting, he replied, "It would be hard if there were not something I could do."
2 CONT
2 CONT As king he followed a steady policy of neutrality in the conflict between France and England, and refused to be tempted by the offers of either into declaring war on the other. In his life he was orderly and retiring, averse from 
2 CONC taking decisions, though not incapable of acting firmly, as when he cut short the dangerous intrigues of his able minister Zenón de Somodevilla y Bengoechea, Marquis of Ensenada by dismissing and imprisoning him. He was called Fer
2 CONC dinand the Learned for his refined pursuits.
2 CONT
2 CONT Shooting and music were his only pleasures, and he was the generous patron of the famous singer Farinelli, whose voice soothed his melancholy.
2 CONT
2 CONT When he came to the throne, Spain found itself in the War of the Austrian Succession which ended without any benefit to Spain. He started his reign by eliminating the influence of the widow Queen Elisabeth of Parma and her group o
2 CONC f Italian courtesans. As king he followed a steady policy of neutrality in the conflict between France and England, and refused to be tempted by the offers of either into declaring war on the other.
2 CONT
2 CONT Prominent figures during his reign were the Marquis of Ensenada, a Francophile; and José de Carvajal y Lancaster, a supporter of the alliance with Great Britain. The fight between both ended in 1754 with the death of Carvajal the 
2 CONC fall of Ensenada, making Ricardo Wall the new powerful man of the monarchy.
2 CONT
2 CONT The most important tasks during the reign of Ferdinand VI were carried out by the Marquis of Ensenada, the Secretary of the Treasury, Navy and Indies. He suggested that the state help modernize the country. To him, this was necess
2 CONC ary to maintain a position of exterior strength so that France and Great Britain would consider Spain as an ally without supposing Spain's renunciation of its claim to Gibraltar.
2 CONT
2 CONT Among his reform projects were:
2 CONT
2 CONT New model of the Treasury suggested by Ensenada in 1749. He proposed substitution of the traditional taxes with a special tax, the cadastre, that weighed the economic capacity of each contributor based on their property holdings. 
2 CONC He also proposed a reduction of subsidies to by the state to the Cortes and the army. The opposition by the nobility caused the abandonment of the project. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The creation of the Giro Real in 1752, a bank favoring the transfer of public and private funds outside of Spain keeping all of the foreign exchanges in the hands of the Royal Treasury, enriching the State. It is considered the pr
2 CONC edecessor to the Bank of San Carlos, introduced during the reign of Charles III. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The stimulation of commerce in the Americas, which tried to end the monopoly in the Indies and eliminate the injustices of colonial commerce. Thus he leaned toward registered ships rather than fleets of ships. The new system consi
2 CONC sted of the substitution of the fleets and galleons so that a Spanish ship, previously authorized, could conduct trade freely in the Americas. This increased the revenues and decreased the fraud. Even so, this system provoked many
2 CONC  protests among merchants in the private sector. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The modernization of the Navy. According to Ensenada, a powerful navy was fundamental to a power with an overseas empire and aspirations of being respected by France and Great Britain. He increased the navy's budget and expanded t
2 CONC he capacity of the shipyards of Cádiz, Ferrol, Cartagena and Havana which marked the starting point of the Spanish naval power in the eighteenth century. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Church relations which were really tense from start of the reign of Philip V because of the recognition of Charles VI as the King of Spain by the Pope. A regalist policy was maintained that pursued as much political as fiscal obje
2 CONC ctives and whose decisive achievement was the Concord of 1753. From this the right of Universal Patronage was obtained from Pope Benedict XIV, giving important economic benefits to Crown and a great control over the clergy.  
2 CONT
2 CONT During the War of the Austrian Succession and the Seven Years' War, Spain reinforced its military might.
2 CONT
2 CONT The main conflict was its confrontation with Portugal over the colony of Sacramento, from which British contraband was transferred down the Río de la Plata. In 1750 José de Carvajal helped Spain and Portugal strike a deal. Portuga
2 CONC l agreed to renounce the colony and its claim to free navigation down the Río de la Plata. In return, Spain ceded to Portugal two regions on the Brazilian border, one in the Amazon and the other to the south, in which seven of the
2 CONC  thirty Jesuit Guaraní towns. The Spanish had to expel the missionaries, generating a conflict with the Guaraní people that lasted eleven years.
2 CONT
2 CONT The conflict over the towns provoked a crisis in the Spanish Court. Ensenada, favorable to the Jesuits, and Father Rávago, confessor of the King and members of the Society of Jesus, were fired, accused of hindering the agreements 
2 CONC with Portugal.
2 CONT
2 CONT The death of his wife Barbara, who had been devoted to him, and who carefully abstained from political intrigue, broke his heart. Between the date of her death in August of 1758 and his own on August 10, 1759, he fell into a state
2 CONC  of prostration in which he would not even dress, but wandered unshaven, unwashed and in a nightgown about his park. The memoirs of the count of Fernan Nuñez give a shocking picture of his deathbed.
2 CONT
2 CONT * https://ancestors.familysearch.org/en/G3ML-TDV/fernando-vi-de-españa-1713-1759
2 CONT * https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Bourbon-114
2 CONT * https://www.madmonarchs.nl/madmonarchs/ferdinand6/ferdinand6_bio.htm
2 CONT * https://www.encyclopedia.com/humanities/encyclopedias-almanacs-transcripts-and-maps/ferdinand-vi-spain-1713-1759
2 CONT * https://monarchies.fandom.com/wiki/Ferdinand_VI,_King_of_Spain
2 CONT * https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/3975/fernando_vi
2 CONT
2 CONT Monarch of Spain. Fourth son of the first marriage of King Philip V of Bourbon. Former Prince of Asturias, he succeeded his father (1746) who had renounced abdication following the death of the legitimate heir Louis in 1724. His c
2 CONC ourt life was severely compromised by the intriguing influence of her stepmother Elisabetta Farnese who was however expelled from the Royal Palace when he ascended the throne. Ferdinand VI hardly ever intervened directly in intern
2 CONC al and foreign politics, entrusting the care to the Marquis of Ensenada and the Secretary of State José de Carvajal y Lancaster who provided to maintain a form of neutrality such as to guarantee good economic and commercial stabil
2 CONC ity. He died after a short but tormented illness aggravated by symptoms of acute paranoia, probably due to the death of his wife Maria Barbara of Braganza.
