2 CONT ===Golden Fleece - Knights: Spanish Branch===
2 CONT * [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philip_of_Parma '''Philip of Parma''']
2 CONT
2 CONT Philip of Bourbon, Duke of Parma (15 March 1720 – 18 July 1765) was duke of Parma from 1748 to 1765.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the fourth child and third son of Philip V of Spain and his wife, Elizabeth Farnese. He was raised in Madrid and as a child showed more interest in music and art than in politics. He was also the 12th Conde de Chinchón Gran
2 CONC dee of Spain First Class with a Coat of Arms of de Bourbon after the alienation with Royal Authorization in 1738 of the 11th Conde Don Jose Sforza-Cesarini, ?th Duque de Canzano, title he later ceded to his brother Louis in 1754.
2 CONT
2 CONT His mother was from the family of Farnese, which had ruled the Duchy of Parma, Piacenza and Guastalla for many generations. The Duchy had been ruled between 1731 and 1736 by his elder brother Charles, but exchanged with Austria fo
2 CONC r The Two Sicilies after the War of Polish Succession. Twelve years later, in the Treaty of Aix-la-Chapelle (1748), Austria lost the Duchy, and Philip became the new Duke, founding the House of Bourbon-Parma.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Duchy of Parma was ruined by many years of warfare, and in 1759 Philip named the abled Frenchmen Guillaume du Tillot as his minister to restore the economy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Philip was an enlightened ruler. He expelled the Jesuits from Parma in 1768. He stimulated education and philosophy, attracting personalities like Étienne Bonnot de Condillac.
2 CONT
2 CONT Philip died unexpectedly on 18 July 1765 in Alessandria, while he was accompanying his daughter Maria Luisa to Spain to be married to Infante Charles.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
