1 NAME Benedict Leonard /Calvert/
2 GIVN Benedict Leonard
2 SURN Calvert
2 _MARNM Calvert
1 NAME Benedict Leonard Calvert, 15th Proprietary Governor of Maryland
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 20 SEP 1700
2 PLAC Surrey
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 1 JUN 1732
2 PLAC North Atlantic Ocean
2 ADDR
1 OCCU Governor or Maryland
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benedict_Leonard_Calvert
2 CONT
2 CONT The Hon. Benedict Leonard Calvert (September 20, 1700 – June 1, 1732) was the 15th Proprietary Governor of Maryland from 1727 through 1731, appointed by his older brother, Charles Calvert, 5th Baron Baltimore (1699–1751). He was n
2 CONC amed after his father, Benedict Calvert, 4th Baron Baltimore (1679–1715). Calvert suffered from tuberculosis and he died of consumption on board the family ship The Charles on 1 June 1732, while travelling back to his home in Engl
2 CONC and, aged just 32.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early life
2 CONT
2 CONT Like many young aristocrats in 18th century England, Calvert was sent on a Grand Tour of Italy, travelling there from 1724-5. During this time he studied Italian architecture and antiquities, collecting many items which were sent 
2 CONC back to the family home at Woodcote Park in Surrey.
2 CONT
2 CONT Governor of Maryland
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1727 the young Benedict Calvert was sent to Maryland by his older brother Lord Baltimore, with instructions to take over the governorship of the colony, replacing his cousin Captain Charles Calvert. The handover of power from c
2 CONC ousin to cousin was not entirely smooth. Captain Calvert insisted on retaining fifty percent of the 3 pence tobacco duty which was his due under legislation passed in 1727. Benedict was not impressed, and his younger brother Cecil
2 CONC ius wrote to him that family opinion in England was appalled at Captain Calvert's behaviour, and "thinks him mad". Lord Baltimore himself wrote that Benedict should receive the full benefit of the tax.
2 CONT
2 CONT Benedict Calvert was replaced as Governor by Samuel Ogle in 1731. On arrival in Maryland, Ogle wrote to Lord Baltimore that his brother was "much worse than I imagined, and which I believe has not been mended very much by the help
2 CONC  of Physik, of which he takes more than anyone I ever knew in my life".
2 CONT
2 CONT Calvert was elected a fellow of the Royal Society in March, 1731.
2 CONT
2 CONT Death from Tuberculosis
2 CONT
2 CONT Calvert suffered from tuberculosis and he died of consumption on board the family ship The Charles on 1 June 1732, while travelling back to his home in England. He was buried at sea. He left an estate worth around ten thousand pou
2 CONC nds sterling, a large sum at the time, to his younger brother Cecilius Calvert.
2 CONT
2 CONT Legacy
2 CONT
2 CONT Calvert had no children of his own, but he was godfather to Elizabeth Calvert, daughter of his cousin Captain Charles Calvert. In his will, which he drew up before leaving Maryland, he left her a slave boy named Osmyn.
2 CONT
2 CONT The town of Leonardtown, Maryland is named in his honor.
2 CONT
