1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ad%C3%A9la%C3%AFde '''Marie Adélaïde de France'''] Marie Adélaïde de France,[1] Daughter of France (Versailles 23 March 1732 - Trieste 27 February 1800), was the fourth daughter and 
2 CONC sixth child of King Louis XV of France and his Queen consort, Maria Leszczyńska. As the daughter of the king, she was a Fille de France. She was referred to as Madame Adélaïde from 1737 to 1755 and from 1759 to her death, and simp
2 CONC ly as Madame from 1755 to 1759.
2 CONT
2 CONT Originally known as Madame Quatrième ("Madame the Fourth"), until the death of her older sister Louise Marie in 1733, she became Madame Troisième, ("Madame the Third"). Adélaïde also possessed the Duchy of Louvois with her sister 
2 CONC Madame Sophie from 1777, and which had been created for them by their nephew Louis XVI, in their own right.
2 CONT
2 CONT She outlived both of her parents and all of her siblings.
2 CONT
2 CONT ====Childhood====
2 CONT Madame Adélaïde was raised at the Palace of Versailles with her older sister, Madame Henriette, in the shadow of her brother, Louis, the Dauphin. Her younger sisters received their education at the Abbaye de Fontevraud.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was put in the care of Marie Isabelle de Rohan, duchesse de Tallard.
2 CONT ====Versailles====
2 CONT
2 CONT Adélaïde was never married: in the late 1740s, by the time she had reached the age when princesses normally married, there were no consorts available in Europe; she preferred to stay unmarried rather than marry someone who was not
2 CONC  a monarch or an heir to a throne.[2] She became the head of the group of the three unmarried, younger sisters who survived into adulthood; the others were Madame Victoire and Madame Sophie. They all found solace in music.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the early 1750s when the health of Madame de Pompadour was deteriorating, Adélaïde, who was a good rider, became the favorite and close companion of her father for a time, and often accompanied him during his riding and amused 
2 CONC him with conversation. Their new close relationship caused rumors that they had an incestuous relationship.[3] A rumor also claimed that Adélaïde was the true mother of Louis de Narbonne (born 1755) by her father.[4] There is noth
2 CONC ing to indicate that these rumors were true, and Adélaïde's new relationship to her father did not last for many years. Adélaïde was described as intelligent and was the only one of the unmarried sisters with political aspirations
2 CONC , and she attempted, without much success, to gain political influence through her father the king, her brother the Dauphin, and eventually her nephew, the next Dauphin.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Madame Adélaïde, as well as her siblings, attempted without success to prevent their father's liaison with Madame de Pompadour, which began in 1745.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was deeply affected by the death of her sister Madame Henriette at the age of twenty-four in 1752, and by the death of her brother, the Dauphin, in 1765. By 1770, Adélaïde and her sisters were described as bitter old hags, who
2 CONC  spent their days gossiping and knitting in their rooms.[6] They seldom dressed properly, merely putting on panniers covered by a coat when leaving their rooms.[7] They did, however, alternate with the Countess of Provence in acco
2 CONC mpanying Marie Antoinette on official assignments.[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT Madame Adélaïde despised her father's last maîtresse-en-titre, Madame du Barry. When the fourteen-year old Marie-Antoinette became Dauphine in 1770, Madame Adélaïde tried to win her support against Mme du Barry, but the empress Ma
2 CONC ria Theresa opposed it. This was a factor which would cause Adélaide to bear subsequent malice toward Marie Antoinette.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the dauphin's death in 1765, followed in 1767 by that of his second spouse, Marie-Josèphe, Madame Adélaïde took custody of the late dauphine's papers, with instructions concerning their son, Louis Auguste, should he become k
2 CONC ing. The papers were opened on 12 May 1774, after the death of Louis XV, who was succeeded by his grandson Louis Auguste as Louis XVI. Three distinguished names were suggested for the position of Prime Minister (Premier Ministre),
2 CONC  that of Jean-Frédéric Phélypeaux, comte de Maurepas, Emmanuel-Armand de Richelieu, duc d'Aiguillon, and Jean-Baptiste de Machault d'Arnouville.
2 CONT [edit] Later life
2 CONT
2 CONT Madame Adélaïde and her sister left Versailles with the rest of the Royal Family on 6 October 1789, the day following the Parisian women's march to Versailles, and the sisters took up residence at the Château de Bellevue.
2 CONT
2 CONT Revolutionary laws against the Catholic Church caused them to leave France for Italy on 20 February 1791. On their way, they were arrested and detained for several days at Arnay-le-Duc before they were allowed to continue their jo
2 CONC urney. They visited their niece Clotilde, sister of Louis XVI, in Turin, and arrived in Rome on 16 April 1791. As a result of the increasing influence of Revolutionary France, they traveled farther afield, moving to Naples in 1796
2 CONC , where Marie Antoinette's sister, Marie Caroline, was queen.
2 CONT
2 CONT They moved to Corfu in 1799, and finally settled in Trieste, where Victoire died of breast cancer. Adélaïde died one year later. Their bodies were returned to France by Louis XVIII at the time of the Bourbon Restoration, and burie
2 CONC d at the Basilica of Saint-Denis.
2 CONT
2 CONT Among her nephews were the kings of France Louis XVI, Louis XVIII, and Charles X, and Ferdinand, Duke of Parma. She had as nieces Madame Élisabeth, Clotilde, Queen Consort of Sardinia, and Queen Maria Luisa of Spain.
2 CONT
