1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://archive.org/details/historyofknoxdav00chic/page/90
2 CONT
2 CONT from West of Shenandoah - Pioneers Along the Fairfax Line Where It Crosses the South Branch Potomac- Before the Revolution ( 975.4 D25s Vol X, No , Spring-Summer 1992)
2 CONT
2 CONT William Welton ( d. ca. 1816?) m. to Agnes /Ann Parsons William was probably born in Virginia. 28 He was named as an heir in his father's will (1764). He was granted deeds in 1777 ( Hampshire county) and in 1787 to a property on P
2 CONC atterson Creek. 29 He was included among Judy's "Heroes in the Revolution," referred to on that list as "Capt. William Welton." 30 He was a taxpayer in Hampshire County in the early 1780's and in Hardy after its partition from Ham
2 CONC pshire. The inventory of William Welton's estate recorded in Hardy Count in 1816, provides the date of death above. 31.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources: 28. The 1772 will of Thomas Parsons, abstracted in Clara Sage & Laura Jones, Early Records, Hampshire Co, Virginia (Now West Virginia) (Delavan, Wisc.: Delavan Republican Printers & Publishers), 1939, p. 218, names Parson
2 CONC s' sons-in-law Robert Cunningham and William Welton ( but the inference is that the daughter's name was Agnes) ; information from Charles Fisher of Van Nuys, CA names William's wife as Ann Parsons; Sage and Jones, op cit, p, 60 na
2 CONC mes William's wife as Ann. 29. Edgar Sims (ed.), Sims Index to Land Grants in West Virginia ( Charleston, W VA: State of West Virginia), 1952,pp. 238 & 269 30. E. L. Judy, "Heroes in the Revolution" , a History of Grant and Hardy 
2 CONC Counties, West Virginia (Charleston, WVA; Charleston Printing Co.), 1951,p. 220. 31. Ross Johnston, West Virginia Estate Settlements (Baltimore: Genealogical Publishing Co.), 1978, p. 142.
