1 TITL Adm. Sir
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Admiral Sir Charles Tyler''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT * Parents: '''Captain Peter Tyler''' and '''Hon. Anna Maria Roper'''. 
2 CONT * Wife 1: Anne Rice
2 CONT * Wife 2: Margaret Leach, daughter of Alexander Leach, on 25 November 1788. 
2 CONT * Death: 28 September 1835.
2 CONT
2 CONT * He was invested as a Knight Grand Cross, Order of the Bath (G.C.B.).
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Admiral Sir Charles Tyler and Anne Rice
2 CONT * Annie Tyler
2 CONT * Caroline Tyler
2 CONT * Emilia Tyler+ b. c 1780, d. 10 Nov 1881
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Admiral Sir Charles Tyler and Margaret Leach
2 CONT * Jane Tyler
2 CONT * Charles Tyler b. c 1790
2 CONT * Vice-Admiral Sir George Tyler+1 b. 28 Dec 1792, d. 4 Jun 1862
2 CONT * Reverend Roper Trevor Tyler+ b. Apr 1801, d. 19 Feb 1885
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charles_Tyler
2 CONT
2 CONT Admiral Sir Charles Tyler, GCB (1760 - 28 September 1835) was a British admiral who gained fame during the Napoleonic Wars as one of the Nelsonic Band of Brothers and a naval officer of great reputation and success who fought at t
2 CONC he battles of Copenhagen and Trafalgar.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early life
2 CONT
2 CONT Tyler was born in 1760, the son of '''Captain Peter Tyler''' of the Oxfordshire Light Infantry and his wife the '''Hon. Anna Maria Roper''', daughter of Henry Roper, 8th Baron Teynham. Securing a place at sea at eleven, Tyler rose
2 CONC  rapidly through the ranks during the American Revolutionary War, although during that period he had little opportunity to distinguish himself. In 1779 he was promoted to lieutenant, jumping to commander in 1782 and retaining his 
2 CONC position throughout the peacetime Navy of 1783 to 1790, when he was made Post captain.
2 CONT
2 CONT French Revolutionary Wars and Napoleonic Wars
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon the outbreak of the French Revolutionary Wars, Tyler was in command of the frigate HMS Meleager during the siege of Toulon and the invasion of Corsica the following year. He first met Nelson in the course of these campaigns, 
2 CONC and was present when the latter lost an eye at the siege of Calvi.
2 CONT
2 CONT Transferring to HMS Diadem soon afterwards, he was present at Lord Hotham's disappointing battle of Genoa and then spent the next several years operating against North African pirates in the frigate HMS Aigle before she was accide
2 CONC ntally wrecked on Plane Island. Returning to the fleet, Tyler was acquitted of blame for the loss of his ship and was given the ship of the line HMS Warrior which he commanded off Cadiz before returning to England and participatin
2 CONC g under Admiral Nelson at the battle of Copenhagen where he was commended for his actions.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the Peace of Amiens Tyler was returned to shore as commander of a unit of Sea Fencibles, but in 1803 was back at sea, commanding the 80-gun HMS Tonnant, which Nelson had captured at the battle of the Nile. Tonnant was a lar
2 CONC ge, modern ship with a heavy payload, capable of taking on the largest enemy ships. Tyler carefully drilled his crew until Tonnant was amongst the most efficient and powerful ships in the fleet. Tyler was specially requested by Ne
2 CONC lson for the Cadiz blockade in 1805, and thus participated in the battle of Trafalgar, although not before he was forced to travel to Naples where his son was under arrest for desertion from the navy (out of love for a ballerina) 
2 CONC and crippling debts. Unknown to Tyler until much later was the fact that Nelson personally paid the young man's debt and used his influence to have him released and reinstated into the Navy albeit with a stern warning about respon
2 CONC sibilities.
2 CONT
2 CONT Trafalgar
2 CONT
2 CONT At Trafalgar, Tyler was third in line from Collingwood's flagship HMS Royal Sovereign, and was thus quickly and intensely engaged in the battle. Tonnant first forced the Spanish Monarca to surrender (although she later attempted t
2 CONC o escape) before colliding with the Algésiras which soon became hopelessly entangled in the British ship's rigging. This resulted in a savage series of close-range bombardments and boarding actions during which Tyler was shot in t
2 CONC he thigh and French Admiral Magon was killed. Once the Algésiras finally surrendered, the Tonnant although badly battered, was still able to attack and capture the San Juan Nepomuceno at the close of the battle.
2 CONT
2 CONT Struggling back to Gibraltar with over 70 casualties on board, including Tyler himself, the Tonnant relied heavily on other ships for aid, and thus was unable to save the sinking Algésiras although the San Juan Nepomuceno did arri
2 CONC ve at Gibraltar thanks to heroic efforts by her Spanish crew and British prize crew. Briefly returning to London to celebrate and recover, Tyler received the thanks of parliament and numerous awards from the nation before returnin
2 CONC g to his ship in the Mediterranean where he continued to serve, overseeing the surrender of the Russian fleet at Lisbon in 1808.
2 CONT
2 CONT Later career
2 CONT
2 CONT His continued service brought more honours, including command of the Cape of Good Hope squadron based in Cape Town, a role which was followed by post-war promotion to rear admiral. In April 1816 he was initiated into the Order of 
2 CONC the Bath. He was named Vice Admiral. Following his elevation to admiral he did not hold any more sea postings, retiring and reaching full Admiral of the White and Knight Grand Cross of the Order of the Bath by his death in Septemb
2 CONC er 1835 in Gloucester, just two months after the death of his wife of many years, Margaret. They were buried together in the St Nicholas Church in Glamorgan where a marble monument still can be seen to their memory.
