1 NOTE !BIRTH - A BIOGRAPHICAL DICTIONARY OF THE MARYLAND LEGISLATURE, VOL I 1635-1789 by Edward Papenfuse, etc.
2 CONT Benedict Calvert was born about 1724 in England; an illegitimate son who was known in childhood as Benedict Swingate.  His father was Charles Calvert, 5th Lord Baltimore (1699-1751); his mother name unknown.
2 CONT
2 CONT He immigrated in 1742 from England; sent by his father and placed under the care of Dr. George Steuart of Annapolis.  He resided in Annapolis, Anne Arundel Co., 1742; "Mt. Airy," Prince George's Co., by 1765.  - Ibid
2 CONT
2 CONT !MAR - ANNE ARUNDEL CO. CHURCH RECORDS OF THE 17TH AND 18TH CENTURIES by F. Edward Wright, p. 101
2 CONT Hon. Benedict Calvert Esq. (Collector of his Majesty's Customs for District of Patuxent) and Elizabeth Calvert (of late Charles Calvert, Esq., former Governor of the Province) m. 21 Apr 1748.  Witnesses: Onorio Razolina, Elizabeth
2 CONC  Razolini and Ariana Brice.
2 CONT
2 CONT Calvert did not sign the Oath of Fidelity of 1778, but remained in Maryland, probably at Mt. Airy, during the Revolution.  His property was not confiscated. - See Birth Reference
2 CONT
2 CONT !DEATH - Ibid
2 CONT He died on Januaary 9, 1788 and is buried under the chancel of St. Thomas Church, Groom, Prince George's Co.  He had at least 18,026 acres in Prince George's, Frederick, Montgomery and Anne Arundel counties, plus 2 lots in Annapol
2 CONC is, 1 lot in Upper Marlboro and 2 lots in Bladensburg.
