1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henry_Edward_Fox
2 CONT
2 CONT General Henry Edward Fox (4 March 1755 – 18 July 1811) was a British Army general. He fought in the American War of Independence. By the end of the war he had risen to colonel and king's aide-de-camp.
2 CONT
2 CONT Later he was lieutenant-governor of Minorca (1799–1801), Commander-in-Chief of all British Mediterranean forces outside Gibraltar (1801–03), Commander-in-Chief, Ireland (1803), Lieutenant-Governor of Gibraltar (1804–06), Commander
2 CONC -in-Chief in the Mediterranean (1806–07) and minister to Sicily.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT As a son of Henry Fox, first Baron Holland and his second wife, (Georgiana) Caroline Fox, née Lennox, he was a younger brother of the politician Charles James Fox (1749–1806).
2 CONT
2 CONT Life
2 CONT
2 CONT He attended Westminster School before being commissioned as a cornet in the 1st dragoon guards in 1770. Soon after that he spent 1 year's leave at the military academy at Strasbourg. After his return he rose to lieutenant (1773) t
2 CONC hen captain (1774).
2 CONT
2 CONT American War of Independence
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1773 he moved to the 38th Regiment of Foot, stationed at Boston, and fought in the American War of Independence (spending 1778-79 on leave in England). By the end of the war he had risen to colonel and king's aide-de-camp, and 
2 CONC he then moved to command the forces in Nova Scotia (1783–89), where he was influential in the creation of the new colony of New Brunswick, and then the Chatham barracks (1789–93).
2 CONT
2 CONT Later career
2 CONT
2 CONT Next he was quartermaster-general on the duke of York's staff in Flanders to replace the recently killed James Moncrieff (1793–95) and fought in the Netherlands theatre of the French Revolutionary Wars. He then served as inspector
2 CONC -general of the recruiting service (1795–99), lieutenant-governor of Minorca (1799–1801), Commander-in-Chief of all British Mediterranean forces outside Gibraltar (1801–03, replacing the killed General Sir Ralph Abercromby at the 
2 CONC battle of Aboukir) and finally Commander-in-Chief, Ireland (1803). In Ireland he was caught off-guard by Robert Emmet's Dublin uprising (22 July 1803) and was quickly replaced by Lieutenant-General Cathcart, whose appointment was 
2 CONC gazetted on 20 October.
2 CONT
2 CONT Fox moved to be commander of the London district (1803), Lieutenant-Governor of Gibraltar (1804–06), Commander-in-Chief in the Mediterranean (1806–07) and minister to Sicily. With his health weakening, Fox passed active command of
2 CONC  the force to his deputy, Lieutenant-General Sir John Moore. The smallness of his force (made yet smaller when Major-General Mackenzie Fraser was sent to occupy Alexandria) meant he refused the repeated requests from the Sicilian 
2 CONC court and William Drummond, British minister at the Sicilian court, for land operations on the Italian mainland. Fox and Moore also opposed the naval commander William Sidney Smith's political machinations at the Sicilian court, c
2 CONC ontrary as they were to the army's tactics for the Italian theatre, until Fox's ill health finally led to his being recalled by the British government and replaced by Moore. Fox was promoted full general on 25 April 1808 and appoi
2 CONC nted governor of Portsmouth in 1810, dying the following year.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and issue
2 CONT
2 CONT On 14 November 1786 he married Marianne Clayton, daughter of William Clayton, 4th Baronet and sister of Catherine, Lady Howard de Walden, and they had 3 children
2 CONT
2 CONT Louisa Amelia Fox (d. 1828), later wife of Major-General Sir Henry Bunbury
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry Stephen Fox, (1791–1846), later the UK's envoy-extraordinary and minister-plenipotentiary to the United States of America
2 CONT
2 CONT Caroline Fox, who married Major-General William Napier
