2 CONT
2 CONT Died 10 March 1826 (aged 58)
2 CONT
2 CONT Lisbon 
2 CONT
2 CONT John VI (13 May 1767 – 10 March 1826) King of the United Kingdom of Portugal, Brazil and the Algarves (later changed to just King of Portugal and the Algarves, after Brazil was recognized independent in 1825) was born in Lisbon in
2 CONC  1767. John was the 27th (or 28th according to some historians) Portuguese monarch.
2 CONT
2 CONT John was the second son of Maria Francisca of Portugal, the then Princess of Brazil and her husband (and uncle), Infante Pedro, her prince consort. His mother ascended the throne of Portugal in 1777. John's elder brother Joseph di
2 CONC ed in 1788, so John became the heir apparent and received the title of prince of Brazil.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1799 John assumed the reins of government as prince regent in the name of his widowed mother, who had declined into mental illness (perhaps due to porphyria). He retained this position until his mother's death in 1816. John had
2 CONC  been brought up in an ecclesiastical atmosphere and, being naturally of a somewhat weak and helpless character, was ill adapted for the responsibilities he was called on to undertake. His wife, Charlotte of Spain, dominated him. 
2 CONC In 1807, Portugal was invaded by France. At the urging of Britain, the whole Portuguese royal family fled to Brazil, accompanied by an escort of British ships. His court in exile was established in Rio de Janeiro.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1816 John was recognized as king of Portugal after his mother's death but he continued to reside in Brazil, which he had raised to the status of a kingdom on 16 December 1815. The consequent spread of dissatisfaction in Portuga
2 CONC l resulted in the peaceful revolution of 24 August 1820, and the proclamation of a constitutional government, to which John swore fidelity on his return to Portugal in 1821. In the same year, and again in 1823, he had to suppress 
2 CONC a rebellion led by his younger son Miguel, whom he was ultimately compelled to banish in 1824.
2 CONT
2 CONT Meanwhile his elder son and heir, Pedro, declared Brazilian independence from Portugal on 7 September 1822, and subsequently declared himself Emperor Pedro I. John refused to recognize Brazilian independence until 29 August 1825, 
2 CONC when he restored Pedro to the succession in the belief that Brazil and Portugal would be reunited in a dual monarchy federation after his own death. John died at Lisbon on 26 March 1826, and was briefly succeeded by Pedro (as King
2 CONC  Pedro IV). Recent tests made to John's intestines, which had been kept buried on a vase, demonstrated that he may have died due to arsenic poisoning. His nemesis, Napoleon, may have also suffered a similar fate.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Portugalin Juhana VI (port. João VI, 13. toukokuuta 1769 Lissabon – 26. maaliskuuta 1826 Lissabon) oli Portugalin kuningas vuosina 1816–1826. Hän pakeni muun kuningasperheen kanssa Brasiliaan Ranskan suorittaman Portugalin miehity
2 CONC ksen aikana 1807.
2 CONT
2 CONT Juhana VI nousi Portugalin kuninkaaksi vuonna 1816 ja palasi Portugaliin vuonna 1821. Hän hallitsi myös Brasiliaa siihen saakka, kunnes sille myönnettiin itsenäisyys vuonna 1825. Kun Juhana VI kuoli vuonna 1826, häntä seurasi Piet
2 CONC ari IV.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lapset: Pedro I, Mikael, Infanta Isabel Maria of Portugal, Maria Isabel of Portugal, Maria Teresa, Princess of Beira, Infanta Maria Francisca of Portugal, Infanta Ana de Jesus Maria of Portugal, Infanta Maria da Assunção of Portug
2 CONC al, Francisco António, Prince of Beira
