1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lord_William_FitzRoy
2 CONT
2 CONT Rear Admiral Lord William FitzRoy KCB (1 June 1782 – 13 May 1857), was an officer of the British Royal Navy who served during the French Revolutionary and Napoleonic Wars, and also as a Member of Parliament.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family background
2 CONT
2 CONT FitzRoy was the third son of Augustus FitzRoy, 3rd Duke of Grafton, by his second wife, Elizabeth, the daughter of the Reverend Sir Richard Wrottesley, Bt.; he was also an uncle of Vice-Admiral Robert FitzRoy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Naval career
2 CONT
2 CONT FitzRoy entered the Navy on 21 April 1794, on board the frigate Phaeton, firstly serving under Captain William Bentinck, and following the battle of the Glorious First of June, under Captain Robert Stopford. He then served abroad 
2 CONC the 74-gun Leviathan, under Lord Hugh Seymour, following him into the 80-gun Sans Pareil, and seeing action at the Battle of Groix on 23 June 1795. After serving in the frigates Niger, Captain Edward Foote; Phoenix, Captain Lawren
2 CONC ce Halsted; and Cambrian, Captain the Hon. Arthur Kaye Legge, in February 1798 he rejoined Captain Foote on board Seahorse, and was present at the action of 27 June 1798 when Seahorse captured the French frigate Sensible in the St
2 CONC rait of Sicily.
2 CONT
2 CONT FitzRoy was promoted to lieutenant on 13 May 1800 into the frigate Penelope, Captain the Honourable Henry Blackwood, in which he witnessed the surrender of Malta in September, and took part in the Egyptian Campaign in mid-1801. On
2 CONC  31 October 1801, he was appointed acting-commander of the sloop Salamine, and after this was confirmed on 7 January 1802, commanded Mutine. He returned to England, and from 26 January 1803 commanded Fairy. FitzRoy was promoted to
2 CONC  post-captain on 3 March 1804, taking command of the frigate Aeolus, and was present at the battle of Cape Ortegal on 4 November 1805, and at the invasion of Martinique in February 1809.
2 CONT
2 CONT In June 1810 he commissioned the frigate Macedonian to serve on the Lisbon station. On 7 April 1811 FitzRoy was dismissed from the Navy after a court-martial found him guilty of "False Expense of Stores" and "Tyranny & Oppression"
2 CONC . FitzRoy was charged with falsifying the reports of the ships stores and selling the surplus for his own profit. He also sentenced a seaman to 48 lashes for drunkenness, four times the legal maximum. Furthermore, when challenged,
2 CONC  FitzRoy accused the master of "contempt" and had him clapped in irons, also in breach of naval law. Despite being declared incapable of ever serving again as an officer, FitzRoy was restored to his former rank and seniority by th
2 CONC e Prince Regent the following August, though he received no further employment in the Navy. Nevertheless, he was made a Companion of the Order of the Bath on 4 June 1815, promoted to rear admiral on 10 January 1837, and made a Kni
2 CONC ght Commander of the Order of the Bath on 4 July 1840.
2 CONT
2 CONT Political career
2 CONT
2 CONT Fitzroy was still on active service in Aeolus when he was elected as Member of Parliament for the family seat of Thetford in the 1806 election, and so did not make first appearance in the house until 1810, as a supporter of the Wh
2 CONC igs. He was replaced as MP by his brother Lord John FitzRoy at the 1812 election.
2 CONT
2 CONT Personal life
2 CONT
2 CONT On 9 August 1816 he married Georgiana, the second daughter of Thomas Raikes, and had a son and three daughters. Admiral FitzRoy died at East Sheen, London, on 13 May 1857
