1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT ===Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Calvert-1042
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Edward Henry Calvert''' was born on November 7, 1766 in Mount Airy, Charles Co, Province of Maryland, son of Benedict Swingate Calvert (1722–1788).
2 CONT
2 CONT Benedict Swingate Calvert and Elizabeth Calvert were first cousins. Edwin H. Calvert grew up on the family farm, Mount Airy, which he later took over. George Washington often visited Mount Airy, and wrote about his trips there. Ca
2 CONC lvert's siblings included Charles Calvert, who was painted by John Hesselius with a slave, Eleanor (Calvert) Stuart (abt.1754-1811), who married John Parke Custis (1754-1781), and George Calvert (1768-1838) of Riversdale. Many of 
2 CONC his other brothers died as children or babies.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT On March 1, 1796, he married Elizabeth Biscoe (born about October 9, 1780 in Maryland, United States; daughter of George Biscoe and Araminta Rousby (Thompson) Biscoe) from St. Mary's County at Mount Airy. The Calvert family did no
2 CONC t accept Biscoe fully. Her husband's sister-in-law Rosalie Stier wrote to her sister that Elizabeth (Biscoe) Calvert (abt.1780-abt.1857) was "both narrow-minded and ill-natured, and no one can tolerate her." 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Calverts had at least eight children, all born and raised at Mount Airy. [1]Two of them were:
2 CONT
2 CONT #     George Calvert (1798–1885) 
2 CONT #     John Calvert (~1810–1869)  
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1809, Edward H. Calvert was chosen to be one of four representatives in the Maryland House of Representatives, so Edward and Elizabeth Calvert had a house in Annapolis in addition to the one in Mount Airy. Even though Edward Ca
2 CONC lvert had a great deal of land, he had financial difficulties. . In 1811, he owed $17,000 to his brother George Calvert of Riversdale. Throughout the years, although Edward's wealth and property were mostly given to his brother Ge
2 CONC orge, Edward H. Calvert was still one of the most important landowners in Prince George's County.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward Calvert owned many slaves. The person in charge of Calvert's plantation was James Gibbs Webster, who was Mary Surratt's grandfather. Calvert had a slave named Ned who fled from Mount Airy in 1794. The following year, he fre
2 CONC ed his slave, Davey. In 1809, he bought three slaves from James Smith. Their names were John, Sam, and Milley. During the War of 1812, he lost four male slaves: Charles, John, Tom, and William. After the fight, Calvert bought Lett
2 CONC y, a slave woman, from the estate of James Harvey. Calvert filed a claim to receive reparations for slaves that fled from his property. In later years he had to mortgage slaves to his brother in addition to the real estate that he
2 CONC  could barely afford. In 1826, he sold a great quantity of his slaves to George Calvert. [1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward died on July 12, 1846 in Maryland, United States, aged 79.[
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth: 	1766 Death: 	1846
2 CONT
2 CONT Sacred to the memory of Edward Henry Calvert 1766-1846, son of Benedict and Elizabeth Calvert; married Mar. 1, 1796, Elizabeth Biscoe 1780-1857, daughter of Gen. George W. Biscoe and Anna Maria Hopewell and family---Removed from M
2 CONC t. Airy cemetery August 15, 1949. This removal made possible by the will of Araminta Calvert Chew:
2 CONT
2 CONT NOTE:  According to "The Ancestry of Rosalie Morris Johnson" as compiled by R. Winder Johnson and published in 1905, the parents of Elizabeth (Biscoe) Calvert were George Biscoe and Araminta (Thompson) Carroll.
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward Henry Calvert 1800 1880 
