1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''George Calvert''' (February 2, 1768 – January 28, 1838), was a plantation owner and slaveholder [1] in late eighteenth- and early nineteenth-century Maryland. His plantation house, Riversdale plantation, also kn
2 CONC own as the Calvert Mansion, is a five-part, large-scale late Georgian mansion with superior Federal interior, built between 1801 and 1807, and was designated a National Historic Landmark in 1997. Calvert's wife, the Belgian-born h
2 CONC eiress Rosalie Stier Calvert, was an indefatigable correspondent whose letters, titled Mistress of Riversdale, The Plantation Letters of Rosalie Stier Calvert, was published by the Johns Hopkins University Press in 1991. The lette
2 CONC rs range in date from 1795 to 1821, and illuminate the life of the Calverts' plantation household during the events leading up to and during the War of 1812.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Early life''' <br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT George Calvert was born at his father's plantation home of Mount Airy, Maryland, on February 2, 1768, the youngest son of Benedict Swingate Calvert, who was himself the illegitimate son of Charles Calvert, 5th Baron Baltimore, the
2 CONC  penultimate Lord Proprietor of the Province of Maryland.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Riversdale''' <br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT George Calvert lived at Riversdale plantation, now a National Historic Landmark.George Calvert lived at the Riversdale plantation, also known as the Calvert Mansion, a five-part, large-scale late Georgian mansion with superior Fed
2 CONC eral interior, built between 1801 and 1807. Also known as Baltimore House, Calvert Mansion or Riversdale Mansion, it is located at 4811 Riverdale Road in Riverdale Park, Maryland. It was designated a National Historic Landmark in 
2 CONC 1997.
2 CONT
2 CONT Once the manor house and centerpiece of a 739-acre (2.99 km2) plantation, Riversdale was built for Belgian émigré Henri Joseph Stier, Baron de Stier, who lived in the Brice House in Annapolis, Maryland immediately prior to buildin
2 CONC g Riversdale. Stier planned the house in 1801 to resemble his Belgian residence, the Chateau du Mick. Four years later, Stier returned to Belgium, leaving the unfinished Riversdale to be completed by his daughter, Rosalie Stier Ca
2 CONC lvert and her husband George.
2 CONT
2 CONT The number of slaves employed at Riversdale varied from around fifteen in 1800 to thirty-two, as reported in the 1806 tax assessment. [3] <br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Family life''' <br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT George and Rosalie Calvert were married on June 11, 1799. His wife was an indefatigable correspondent and a collection of her letters, titled Mistress of Riversdale, The Plantation Letters of Rosalie Stier Calvert, was published b
2 CONC y the Johns Hopkins University Press in 1991, edited by Margaret Law Callcott. The letters range in date from 1795 to 1821, and illuminate the life of Stier's plantation household during the events leading up to and during the War
2 CONC  of 1812.
2 CONT
2 CONT The couple had a large family. Their son Charles Benedict Calvert established the Maryland Agricultural College, now the University of Maryland, College Park, on part of the Riversdale property. Another son, George Henry Calvert (
2 CONC January 2, 1803 - May 24, 1889) was a noted editor, essayist, dramatist, poet, and biographer.
2 CONT
2 CONT * Caroline Maria Calvert (July 15, 1800 - November 25, 1842), married on June 19, 1823 Thomas Willing Morris of Philadelphia.
2 CONT * George Henry Calvert (1803–1889), essayist, dramatist, poet, and biographer. 
2 CONT * Marie Louise Calvert (1804–1809), died in childhood. 
2 CONT * Rosalie Eugenia Calvert, of Goodwood, Maryland (Riversdale, Bladensburg, Maryland, October 19, 1806 - Goodwood, May 6, 1845), married in Philadelphia on November 11, 1830 Charles Henry Carter (England, January 29, 1804 - July 5,
2 CONC  1892). 
2 CONT * Charles Benedict Calvert (1808–1864), was a U.S. Congressman who founded what is now the University of Maryland, College Park, chartered in 1856.[3][4] 
2 CONT * Marie Louise Calvert (1812–1813), died in infancy. 
2 CONT * Julia Calvert, of Cedar Grove, Virginia (Riversdale, Bladensburg, January 31, 1814 - Cedar Grove, June 8, 1888), married in Riversdale on May 7, 1833 Richard Henry Stuart (Pop's Creek, May 31, 1808 - Cedar Grove, May 14, 1889). 
2 CONT * Amelia Isabella Calvert (1817–1820), died in infancy. 
2 CONT * Rosalie herself died on March 13, 1821, according to her physician, "of a general dropsy affecting the whole system", at the relatively young age of 43.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Politics''' <br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1816 Calvert was approached by Maryland Federalists who asked him to run as the Federalist candidate for Governor of Maryland. Calvert came from a long line of politicians; his ancestors had ruled the Province of Maryland. Howe
2 CONC ver, pressed by his wife Rosalie to avoid politics, he was persuaded to devote his energies instead to the economic well-being of his family. However, it seems he may have missed an opportunity, as the Federalist candidate chosen 
2 CONC in his place, Charles Carnan Ridgely was duly elected that year.[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Founding of the Washington Jockey Club''' <br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1802 the Club sought a new sight for the tract, as the current one that lay the rear of what is now the site of Decatur House at H Street and Jackson Place, crossing Seventeenth Street and Pennsylvania Avenue to Twentieth Stree
2 CONC t-today the Eisenhower Executive Office Building-was being overtaken be the growth of the Federal City. With the leadership of John Tayloe III and Charles Carnan Ridgely and support of Gen. John Peter Van Ness, Dr. William Thornto
2 CONC n, G.W. P. Custis, John D. Threlkeld of Georgetown and George Calvert of Riversdale, Bladensburg, Maryland, the contests were moved to Meridian Hill, south of Columbia Road between Fourteenth and Sixteenth Streets, and were conduc
2 CONC ted at the Holmstead Farm's one mile oval track. [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_Calvert_(planter) Wikipedia]
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Here lies the body of George Calvert, Esq., of Riversdale, youngest son of Benediict Calvert, Esq., of Mount Airy, Prince George's County, Maryland, and grandson of Charles Calvert, sixth Lord Baltimore, who died January 28, 1838 
2 CONC aged 70.
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: The tombstone is in error for Charles Calvert was Fifth Lord Baltimore.
2 CONT * Reference: [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/33375575/george-calvert Find A Grave Memorial] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Jun 23 2019, 15:54:53 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT
