1 NOTE Info from Greg NELSON, Salem, OR.
1 NOTE !BIRTH - ACROSS THE YEARS IN PRINCE GEORGE'S CO. by Effie Gwynn Bowie, p.106
2 CONT GEORGE CALVERT, son of Benedict (1724-88) was born at Mt. Airy, February 2, 1768; died at Riversdale January 28, 1838.
2 CONT
2 CONT MISTRESS OF RIVERSDALE, THE PLANTATION LETTERS OF ROSALIE STIER CALVERT 1795-1821 Edited & Translated by Margaret Law Callcott, p. 269, n. 5
2 CONT Benedict Calvert (ca. 1724-1788), George Calvert's father, was the illegitimate but acknowledged son of Charles Calvert, fifth Lord Baltimore.  His mother is unknown.  It has been suggested that his mother was Petronille Melusina 
2 CONC Schulenberg, countess of Walsingham (1693-1778), one of three natural daughters of George I and his morganic wife, the duchess of Kendall.  Lady Walsingham married the earl of Chesterfield in 1733.  It should be noted, however, th
2 CONC at the connection between Benedict Calvert and the countess of Walsingham in this article rests on "family letters" whose location is not given.  Benedict was sent to America by his father in 1742 and placed under the care of Dr. 
2 CONC George Steuart (ca. 1700-1784) of Annapolis.  The Prince of Wales in 1742 was Frederick Louis (1707-1751), son of George II and father of George III, but there is no independent confirmation of his role in this voyage.  Charles Ca
2 CONC lvert, fifth Lord Baltimore, served as cofferer to the Prince of Wales and named his legitimate son and successor after him. (See John Bailey Calvert Nicklin, THE CALVERT FAMILY - Maryland Hist. Magazine 16 (1921), 313-18.
2 CONT
2 CONT !MAR - MISTRESS OF RIVERSDALE PLANTATION, LETTERS OF  ROSALIE STIER CALVERT 1795-1821 - THE STIER-CALVERT FAMILY
2 CONT George Calvert married Rosalie Eugenia Stier on June 11, 1799.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1809 the Calverts owned 91 slaves, up from the 76 they had owned when they married 10 years before.  There were more slaves at the Mount Albion plantation, which had 58, than at Riversdale, with 33 at this time; this, of course
2 CONC , was due to the more labor-entensive demands of tobacco cultibation, the principal crop at Mount Albion.  Less than half of these slaves, however, were of optimun working and reproductive age, considered to be from age 14 to 45 f
2 CONC or men and 14 to 36 for women.  Mount Albion had 26 and Riversdale had 16 slaves in the optimum category (prince George's Co. Tax Assessments, Personal Property, 1809, folls. 25, 29). - Ibid p. 223-224
2 CONT
2 CONT !DEATH - MARRIAGES AND DEATHS FROM THE MARYLAND GAZETTE, 1727-1839 compiled by Robert Barnes, p. 26
2 CONT CALVERT, George, died Sun., at his residence near Bladensburg, P. G. Co., aged 70 years, less four days, leaving seven children. (Feb. 1, 1838)
2 CONT
2 CONT BALTIMORE CITY DEATHS AND BURIALS 1834-1840 by Henry C. Peden, Jr., p. 51
2 CONT CALVERT, George, d. Jan. 28, 1838 (Ref. Hollowak's Index)
2 CONT
2 CONT !BURIAL - ACROSS THE YEARS IN PRINCE GEORGE'S CO. by Effie Gwynn Bowie, p.422
2 CONT Tombstone reads:  "Here lies the body of George Calvert, Esq., of Mt. Airy, Prince George's Co., MD., grandson of Charles, fifth Lord Baltimore, who died January 28, 1838, aged ___?
2 CONT
2 CONT GEORGE CALVERT"S OTHER FAMILY 
2 CONT Like many other slaveowners, like some of his own ancestors, George Calvert had another family.  We can be sure of at least one slave mistress, and he probably had others.  There were children from his liaisons and evidence that R
2 CONC osalie knew of his relationships with his female slaves.  His relationship with his slave mistress, Eleanor Beckett began long before he met Rosale Stier.  After his father's death in 1788, George, age 20, became master of Mt. Alb
2 CONC ion plantation, and there he spent a long bachelorhood, not marrying Rosalie until he was 31.  At Mt. Albion Calvert began his affair with Eleanor Beckett.  After his own marriage, he arranged a suitable marriage for Eleanor.  Whe
2 CONC n she was widowed, he moved her and her family to Mntgomery Co. and made costly arrangements for their well-being.  Like his Lord Baltimore ancestors, he did not deny his illegitimate offspring or the woman who had borne them. - M
2 CONC ISTRESS OF RIVERSDALE, THE PLANTATION LETTERS OF ROSALIE STIER CALVERT 1795-1821 Edited & Translated by Margaret Law Callcott, p. 178-179
2 CONT
2 CONT George Calvert, in his will (P.C. 89 W.M.) 8 June 1835; 3 February 1838 (probated), devised the whole of his property to his two sons, George Henry and Charles Benedict Calvert and appointed them executors.  George Calvert renounc
2 CONC ed the executorship. - See Birth reference.
