1 NOTE {geni:about_me}     Sir Frederick Morton Eden, 2nd Baronet, of Maryland (18 June 1766 – 14 November 1809) was an English writer on poverty and pioneering social investigator.
2 CONT
2 CONT     1 Early life
2 CONT
2 CONT     Early life
2 CONT     Frederick Morton Eden was the eldest son of Sir Robert Eden, 1st Baronet, of Maryland, and his wife Caroline Calvert, sister of the last Lord Baltimore and niece of Thomas Bladen's wife. His father was governor of Maryland and
2 CONC  was created a baronet in 1776. Frederick inherited the baronetcy on the death of his father in 1784. Eden studied at Christ Church, Oxford. He was one of the founders of the Globe Insurance Company and later its chairman. 1803 [1
2 CONC ] He died suddenly at the office of the company at the early age of 43.
2 CONT     Career
2 CONT
2 CONT     Eden’s reputation as a social investigator rests on The State of the Poor, published in 3 volumes in 1797. He explained the circumstances that led him to do the research
2 CONT
2 CONT         "The difficulties which the labouring classes experienced, from the high price of grain, and of provisions in general, as well as of cloathing [sic] and fuel, during the years 1794 and 1795, induced me, from motives both o
2 CONC f benevolence and personal curiosity, to investigate their conditions in various parts of the kingdom."
2 CONT
2 CONT     The book was intended to provide a factual basis for the current debate on what to do about the poor. Eden writes at the beginning of the book
2 CONT
2 CONT         "These and many similar questions [relating to the poor laws] cannot, as it seems to me, be fully and satisfactorily answered, unless many minute circumstances are previously stated, which have been rarely sufficiently att
2 CONC ended to in the plausible and ingenious but unsolid speculations of several merely theoretic reasoners."
2 CONT
2 CONT     In the style of the time, the full title of the book is a catalogue of its contents:
2 CONT
2 CONT         "The State of the Poor: or a history of the labouring classes in England, from the Conquest to the present period; in which are particularly considered, their domestic economy, with respect to diet, dress, fuel, and habita
2 CONC tion; and the various plans which, from time to time, have been proposed and adopted for the relief of the poor: together with parochial reports relative to the administration of work-houses, and houses of industry; the state of t
2 CONC he Friendly Societies, and other public institutions; in several agricultural, commercial and manufacturing, districts. With a large appendix; containing a comparative and chronological table of the prices of labour, of provisions
2 CONC , and of other commodities; an account of the poor in Scotland; and many original documents on subjects of national importance."
2 CONT
2 CONT     Eden did some of the field-work himself, obtained information from clergymen but also sent out "a remarkably faithful and intelligent person" with a questionnaire he had designed. This was modelled on that used by Sir John Sin
2 CONC clair in his Statistical Account of Scotland.
2 CONT     Influence and legacy
2 CONT
2 CONT     In The Literature of Political Economy McCulloch wrote of Eden's work "Altogether, this is the grand storehouse of information respecting the labouring classes of England, and should have a prominent place in every library."
2 CONT
2 CONT     Karl Marx wrote that Eden was "the only disciple of Adam Smith during the eighteenth century that produced any work of any importance"' (DNB).
2 CONT
2 CONT     Edgeworth wrote the article on Eden in the original Palgrave Dictionary. He considered that the book entitled Eden to "rank with Arthur Young as one of those immediate successors of Adam Smith who best developed the inductive 
2 CONC branch of political economy."
2 CONT
2 CONT     The twentieth-century British Prime Minister Anthony Eden was a kinsman and, according to a recent biography (D.R. Thorpe Eden: The Life and Times of Anthony Eden ), he was influenced by F. M. Eden's work and kept a copy on hi
2 CONC s shelves throughout his life.
2 CONT     Family life
2 CONT
2 CONT     Eden married Anne Smith on 10 January 1792. They had seven children:
2 CONT         Marianne Eden (c1793-13 May 1859)
2 CONT         Sir Frederick Eden, 3rd Baronet (c1794-24 December 1814)
2 CONT         Caroline Eden (c1801 - 10 November 1854)
2 CONT         Sir William Eden, 4th/6th Bt.+1 b. 31 Jan 1803, died 21 Oct 1873)
2 CONT         Rt Reverend Robert Eden (2 September 1804 - 26 Aug 1886)
2 CONT         Lieutenant-General George Morton Eden (10 May 1806 - November 1862)
2 CONT         Vice-Admiral Sir Charles Eden (3 July 1808 - 7 Mar 1878) [2]
2 CONT     Colonial Families of the USA, 1607-1775
2 CONT     England, Pallot's Marriage Index, 1780-1837
2 CONT     Dictionary of National Biography, Volumes 1-22
2 CONT
2 CONT wikipedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Candy
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Candy originally shared this on 08 Dec 2015 Linked To Saved by (10 of 13)
2 CONT
2 CONT Comments http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sir_Frederick_Morton_Eden
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Frederick Morton Eden, 2nd Baronet, of Maryland (18 June 1766 – 14 November 1809) was an English writer on poverty and pioneering social investigator.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early life
2 CONT
2 CONT Frederick Morton Eden was the eldest son of Sir Robert Eden, 1st Baronet, of Maryland, and his wife Caroline Calvert, sister of the last Lord Baltimore and niece of Thomas Bladen's wife. His father was governor of Maryland and was
2 CONC  created a baronet in 1776. Frederick inherited the baronetcy on the death of his father in 1784. Eden studied at Christ Church, Oxford. He was one of the founders of the Globe Insurance Company and later its chairman. 1803  He di
2 CONC ed suddenly at the office of the company at the early age of 43.
2 CONT
2 CONT Career
2 CONT
2 CONT Eden’s reputation as a social investigator rests on The State of the Poor, published in 3 volumes in 1797. He explained the circumstances that led him to do the research
2 CONT
2 CONT "The difficulties which the labouring classes experienced, from the high price of grain, and of provisions in general, as well as of cloathing [sic] and fuel, during the years 1794 and 1795, induced me, from motives both of benevo
2 CONC lence and personal curiosity, to investigate their conditions in various parts of the kingdom."
2 CONT
2 CONT The book was intended to provide a factual basis for the current debate on what to do about the poor. Eden writes at the beginning of the book
2 CONT
2 CONT "These and many similar questions [relating to the poor laws] cannot, as it seems to me, be fully and satisfactorily answered, unless many minute circumstances are previously stated, which have been rarely sufficiently attended to
2 CONC  in the plausible and ingenious but unsolid speculations of several merely theoretic reasoners."
2 CONT
2 CONT In the style of the time, the full title of the book is a catalogue of its contents:
2 CONT
2 CONT "The State of the Poor: or a history of the labouring classes in England, from the Conquest to the present period; in which are particularly considered, their domestic economy, with respect to diet, dress, fuel, and habitation; an
2 CONC d the various plans which, from time to time, have been proposed and adopted for the relief of the poor: together with parochial reports relative to the administration of work-houses, and houses of industry; the state of the Frien
2 CONC dly Societies, and other public institutions; in several agricultural, commercial and manufacturing, districts. With a large appendix; containing a comparative and chronological table of the prices of labour, of provisions, and of
2 CONC  other commodities; an account of the poor in Scotland; and many original documents on subjects of national importance."
2 CONT
2 CONT Eden did some of the field-work himself, obtained information from clergymen but also sent out "a remarkably faithful and intelligent person" with a questionnaire he had designed. This was modelled on that used by Sir John Sinclai
2 CONC r in his Statistical Account of Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Influence and legacy
2 CONT
2 CONT In The Literature of Political Economy McCulloch wrote of Eden's work "Altogether, this is the grand storehouse of information respecting the labouring classes of England, and should have a prominent place in every library."
2 CONT
2 CONT Karl Marx wrote that Eden was "the only disciple of Adam Smith during the eighteenth century that produced any work of any importance"' (DNB).
2 CONT
2 CONT Edgeworth wrote the article on Eden in the original Palgrave Dictionary. He considered that the book entitled Eden to "rank with Arthur Young as one of those immediate successors of Adam Smith who best developed the inductive bran
2 CONC ch of political economy."
2 CONT
2 CONT The twentieth-century British Prime Minister Anthony Eden was a kinsman and, according to a recent biography (D.R. Thorpe Eden: The Life and Times of Anthony Eden ), he was influenced by F. M. Eden's work and kept a copy on his sh
2 CONC elves throughout his life.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family life
2 CONT
2 CONT Eden married Anne Smith on 10 January 1792. They had seven children:
2 CONT
2 CONT  Marianne Eden (c1793-13 May 1859)
2 CONT
2 CONT  Sir Frederick Eden, 3rd Baronet (c1794-24 December 1814)
2 CONT
2 CONT  Caroline Eden (c1801 - 10 November 1854)
2 CONT
2 CONT  Sir William Eden, 4th/6th Bt.+1 b. 31 Jan 1803, died 21 Oct 1873)
2 CONT
2 CONT  Rt Reverend Robert Eden (2 September 1804 - 26 Aug 1886)
2 CONT
2 CONT  Lieutenant-General George Morton Eden (10 May 1806 - November 1862)
2 CONT
2 CONT  Vice-Admiral Sir Charles Eden (3 July 1808 - 7 Mar 1878) 
