1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_Craven,_1st_Earl_of_Craven,_7th_Baron_Craven
2 CONT
2 CONT Major-General William Craven, 1st Earl of Craven (28 September 1770 – 30 July 1825) was a British soldier.
2 CONT
2 CONT Life
2 CONT
2 CONT Craven was the eldest son of William Craven, 6th Baron Craven, and succeeded his father as seventh Baron Craven in 1791. He served in the Army and achieved the rank of major-general. In 1801 he was created Viscount Uffington, in t
2 CONC he County of Berkshire, and Earl of Craven, in the County of York. The earldom was a revival of the title held by his 17th-century kinsman and namesake William Craven, 1st Earl of Craven.
2 CONT
2 CONT Craven later served as Lord Lieutenant of Berkshire from 1819 to 1826. He mostly resided at Coombe Abbey, near Coventry in Warwickshire and occasionally at Hamstead Marshall in Berkshire. He is not entirely forgotten — Harriette W
2 CONC ilson begins her famous memoir, "I shall not say why and how I became, at the age of fifteen, the mistress of the Earl of Craven."
2 CONT
2 CONT Craven married Louisa Brunton in 1807. He died in July 1825, aged 54, and was succeeded in his titles by his son William.
