2 DATE 14 JUN 1809
1 DEAT
2 DATE 17 JAN 1904
1 TITL Admiral-of-the-Fleet Sir
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henry_Keppel
2 CONT
2 CONT Admiral of the Fleet Sir Henry Keppel, GCB, OM (14 June 1809 – 17 January 1904) was a British admiral, son of the 4th Earl of Albemarle and of his wife Elizabeth, daughter of Lord de Clifford.
2 CONT
2 CONT Naval career
2 CONT
2 CONT He entered the navy from the old naval academy of Portsmouth in 1822.[1] He became lieutenant in 1829 and commander in 1833.[1] His first command was largely passed on the coast of Spain, which was then in the midst of the convuls
2 CONC ions of the Carlist War. Captain Keppel had already made himself known as a good seaman. He was engaged with the squadron stationed on the west coast of Africa to suppress the slave trade.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1837 he was promoted post captain,[1] and appointed in 1841 to the service in China[1] and against the Malay pirates, a service which he repeated in 1847, when in command of HMS Maeander.
2 CONT
2 CONT In May 1853 he was appointed to the command of the new steam line-of-battle ship HMS St Jean d'Acre.[1] When the Crimean War broke out on 1854, the St Jean d'Acre formed part of the Baltic Fleet.[1] Keppel witnessed the fall of Bo
2 CONC marsund. In 1855, St Jean d'Acre was sent to the Black Sea. [3] On 21 July 1855, Keppel swapped commands with the captain of the sailing line-of-battle ship Rodney,[1] whose crew was all ashore. Keppel served as commander of the N
2 CONC aval Brigade which was besieging Sebastopol.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT After the Crimean War he was again sent out to China, this time in command of the Raleigh,[1] as commodore to serve under Sir M. Seymour. The Raleigh was lost on an uncharted rock near Hong Kong, but three small vessels were named
2 CONC  to act as her tenders, and Commodore Keppel commanded in them, and with the crew of the Raleigh, in the action with the Chinese at the Battle of Fatshan Creek (June 1, 1857). He was honorably acquitted for the loss of the Raleigh
2 CONC , and was given command of HMS Alligator, which be held until his promotion to rear-admiral. For his share in the action at the Battle of Fatshan Creek he was made KCB.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT The prevalence of peace gave Sir Henry Keppel no further chance of active service, but he became Commander-in-Chief, Cape of Good Hope and West Coast of Africa Station in 1860, Commander-in-Chief, China Station in 1867 and Command
2 CONC er-in-Chief, Plymouth in 1872.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT He retired from the active list in 1879,[1] two years after he attained the rank of admiral of the fleet.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Keppel married twice: firstly Katherine Louisa Crosbie, daughter of General Sir John Crosbie on 25 February 1839 and secondly Jane Elizabeth West, daughter of Martin John West on 31 October 1861. He had one son, Colin Richard Kepp
2 CONC el, and one daughter by his second wife. Keppel Harbour in Singapore is named after him. He lived at Grove Lodge at Winkfield Row in Berkshire and is buried in the parish church at Winkfield.
2 CONT
2 CONT Keppel's relationship with Lady Eliza Lucy Grey was discovered by her husband Sir George Grey in 1860, and this, together with accusations of infidelity against Sir George Grey, led to the breakdown of their marriage. [5]
