1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Russell,_1st_Earl_Russell
2 CONT
2 CONT John Russell, 1st Earl Russell, KG, GCMG, PC (18 August 1792 – 28 May 1878), known as Lord John Russell before 1861, was an English Whig and Liberal politician who served twice as Prime Minister of the United Kingdom in the mid-19
2 CONC th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT Russell was born into the highest echelons of the British aristocracy. The Russell family had been one of the principal Whig dynasties in England since the 17th century, and were among the richest handful of aristocratic landownin
2 CONC g families in the country, but as a younger son of the 6th Duke of Bedford he was not in line to inherit the family estates.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was educated at Westminster School and the University of Edinburgh, which he attended for three years but did not take a degree.[1] He is one of only five university-educated British Prime Ministers to have not attended Oxford 
2 CONC or Cambridge (the others being the Earl of Bute, Andrew Bonar Law, Neville Chamberlain and Gordon Brown.)
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Politics
2 CONT
2 CONT Russell entered parliament as a Whig in 1813. In 1819, Russell embraced the cause of parliamentary reform, and led the more reformist wing of the Whigs throughout the 1820s. When the Whigs came to power in 1830 in Earl Grey's gove
2 CONC rnment, Russell entered the government as Paymaster of the Forces, and was soon elevated to the Cabinet. He was one of the principal leaders of the fight for the Reform Act 1832, earning the nickname Finality Jack from his complac
2 CONC ency pronouncing the Act a final measure. In 1834, when the leader of the Commons, Lord Althorp, succeeded to the peerage as Earl Spencer, Russell became the leader of the Whigs in the Commons, a position he maintained for the res
2 CONC t of the decade, until the Whigs fell from power in 1841. In this position, Russell continued to lead the more reformist wing of the Whig party, calling, in particular, for religious freedom, and, as Home Secretary in the late 183
2 CONC 0s, played a large role in democratizing the government of British cities (other than London).
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1845, as leader of the Opposition, Russell came out in favour of repeal of the Corn Laws, forcing Conservative Prime Minister Sir Robert Peel to follow him. When the Conservatives split the next year over this issue, the Whigs 
2 CONC returned to power and Russell became Prime Minister. Russell's premiership was frustrating, and, due to party disunity and his own ineffectual leadership, he was unable to get many of the measures he was interested in passed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Russell's first government coincided with the Great Irish Famine of the late 1840s. Russell's government also saw conflict with his headstrong Foreign Secretary, Lord Palmerston, whose belligerence and support for continental revo
2 CONC lution he found embarrassing. When, without royal approval, Palmerston recognized Napoleon III's coup of 2 December 1851, Palmerston was forced to resign, and the ministry soon collapsed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Portrait of John Russell by Francis Grant, 1853
2 CONT
2 CONT After a short-lived minority Conservative government under the Earl of Derby, Russell brought the Whigs into a new coalition government with the Peelite Conservatives, headed by the Peelite Lord Aberdeen. Russell served again as L
2 CONC eader of the House of Commons, and together with Palmerston was instrumental in getting Britain involved in the Crimean War, against the wishes of the cautious, Russophile Aberdeen. Incompetence in the early stages of the war, how
2 CONC ever, led to the collapse of the government, and Palmerston formed a new government. Although Russell was initially included, he did not get on well with his former subordinate, and temporarily retired from politics in 1855, focus
2 CONC ing on writing.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1859, following another short-lived Conservative government, Palmerston and Russell made up their differences, and Russell consented to serve as Foreign Secretary in a new Palmerston cabinet - usually considered the first true 
2 CONC Liberal Cabinet. This period was a particularly eventful one in the world outside Britain, seeing the Unification of Italy, the American Civil War, and the 1864 war over Schleswig-Holstein between Denmark and the German states. Ru
2 CONC ssell's handling of these crises was not particularly noteworthy, and he was always overshadowed by his more eminent chief. In particular, his attempts to attain British mediation in the American war, which were shot down by the c
2 CONC autious Palmerston, did not improve his position. Russell was elevated to the peerage as Viscount Amberley, of Amberley in the County of Gloucester and of Ardsalla in the County of Meath, and Earl Russell, of Kingston Russell in t
2 CONC he County of Dorset, in 1861.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Palmerston suddenly died in late 1865, Russell again became Prime Minister. His second premiership was short and frustrating, and Russell failed in his great ambition of expanding the franchise - a task that would be left to 
2 CONC his Conservative successors, Derby and Benjamin Disraeli. In 1866, party disunity again brought down his government, and Russell went into permanent retirement.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Legacy
2 CONT
2 CONT He was succeeded as Liberal leader by former Peelite William Ewart Gladstone, and was thus the last true Whig to serve as Prime Minister. He may have served as Anthony Trollope's model for the character of Plantagenet Palliser. An
2 CONC  ideal statesman, said Trollope, should have "unblemished, unextinguishable, inexhaustible love of country...But he should also be scrupulous, and, as being scrupulous, weak."[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Among Russell's descendants is the renowned philosopher Bertrand Russell, his grandson.
2 CONT
2 CONT The 1832 Reform Act and the democratisation of the government of British cities are partly attributed to his efforts.
2 CONT
2 CONT He also worked for emancipation, leading the attack on the Test and Corporation acts, which were repealed in 1828, as well as towards legislation limiting working hours in factories in the 1847 Factory Act, and the Public Health A
2 CONC ct of 1848.
2 CONT
2 CONT His government's approach to dealing with the Irish Potato Famine is now widely condemned as counterproductive, ill-informed and disastrous; however, it has been argued that Russell himself (a "Foxite" populist) was sympathetic to
2 CONC  the plight of the Irish poor, and that many of his relief proposals were blocked by his cabinet and the British Parliament.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Russell's governments
2 CONT
2 CONT First Russell ministry (July 1846–February 1852)
2 CONT
2 CONT Second Russell ministry (October 1865–June 1866)
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Literature
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1819 Lord John Russell published his book "Life of Lord Russell" about his famous ancestor. Between 1853 and 1856, he edited the Memoirs, Journal and Correspondence of Thomas Moore, which was published by Longman, Brown, Green 
2 CONC and Longmans over 8 volumes. [3] [4]
2 CONT
2 CONT A Tale of Two Cities by Charles Dickens was dedicated to Lord John Russell "In remembrance of many public services and private kindnesses."
2 CONT == Other References ==
