1 NOTE {geni:about_me} José Miguel de la Cueva y de la Cerda ( Madrid , December 26, 1775 - London , February 18, 1811) was a Spanish aristocrat , soldier and diplomat , holder of the House of Alburquerque and the House of Siruela , note
2 CONC d for his role in the War of Independence , in the battle of Portazgo in the defense of Cádiz.
2 CONT
2 CONT Born in Madrid in 1775, he was the son of Miguel de la Cueva and Enríquez de Navarra , 13th Duke of Alburquerque , 12th Marquis of Cuéllar and IV of la Mina , 16th Count of Siruela , 13th of Ledesma , 13th of Huelma and 7th of Pez
2 CONC uela de las Torres , three times great of Spain , and his wife Cayetana María de la Cerda and Cernesio Odescalchi, of the counts of Parcent .
2 CONT
2 CONT He was lieutenant general of the Royal Armies, knight of the Order of Santiago , commander of Villoria, grand cross of the Order of Charles III and gentleman of the chamber with exercise and servitude of Charles IV . During the co
2 CONC urse of the War of Independence he participated in several contests, his performance in the defense of the Plaza de Cádiz being notable. After the disagreements that arose with the Military Junta, he was sent as ambassador in Lond
2 CONC on to George III of England , where he was very well received and became friends with Arthur Wellesley , Duke of Wellington . From there he sent his well-known Manifesto of the Duke of Alburquerque about his conduct with the Junta
2 CONC  of Cádiz and the arrival of his army in that place . He died in office on February 8, 1811, and his funeral was attended by all the British ministers, the ambassadors, foreign ministers, the English and foreign nobility and, abov
2 CONC e all, the Spanish residents in London.
2 CONT
2 CONT He had married Escolástica Gutiérrez de los Ríos y Sarmiento, daughter of the VI counts of Fernán Nuñez , from whom he only had a daughter, who could not succeed to her father's noble titles, and two natural children outside of hi
2 CONC s marriage, who Because they were not legitimate, they did not have inheritance rights either. For this reason, a long lawsuit of more than twenty years began, which granted the best right of succession to Nicolás Osorio y Zayas ,
2 CONC  Marquis of Alcañices , as the closest relative because he was a descendant of Ana Catalina de la Cueva y de la Cerda , VI Marchioness. from Cadreita (daughter of Francisco Fernández de la Cueva , 10th Duke) whose daughter María D
2 CONC ominga Spínola de la Cueva married Manuel Juan Osorio Velasco Guzmán y Vega , 14th Marquis of Alcañices.
