0 @N6000000002176689388@ NOTE He was the last of the Holy Roman Emperors (1792-1806) dating back to 800 and
1 CONT Charles I, or Charlemagne of France. King of Hungary (1792-1830) and Bohemia
1 CONT (1792-1835), he received his education from his uncle, Emperor Joseph II, who
1 CONT didn't like his nephew's unimaginative outlook and stubbornness, but praised
1 CONT his application & sense of duty. Francis inherited the problems of the French
1 CONT Revolution when Napoleon dictated dissolution of the Holy Roman Empire, and
1 CONT Francis abdicated in 1806.
0 @N6000000002188409060@ NOTE
1 CONT
1 CONT      The official "Great Title" of the Emperor Franz II in 1806 gave equal time to all his possessions, large and small. Francis was "Emperor of Austria; King of Jerusalem, Hungary, Bohemia, Dalmatia, Croatia, Slavonia, Galicia, a
1 CONC nd Ludomiria; Archduke of Austria; Duke of Lorraine,. Salzburg, Wurzburg, Franken, Styria, Carinthia and Carniola ; Grand Duke of Cracow; Prince of Transylvania; Margrave of Moravia; Duke of Sandomir, Masovia, Lublin, Upper and Lo
1 CONC wer Silesia, Auschwitz and Zator, Teschen and Friule; Prince of Berchtesgaden and Mergentheim; Princely Count of Habsburg, Goritz and Gradisca; and Margrave of Upper and Lower Lausitz and Istria." Some of these possessions were la
1 CONC ter lost--notably those in Germany and Italy--and others added, like Bosnia.  The Holy Roman Empire was dissolved in 1806 by Napoleon, and Francis II became Francis I, Emperor of Austria, a title he had created for himself in 1804
1 CONC  anticipating the dissolution of the Holy Roman Empire.
