0 @N6000000002176686787@ NOTE Made heir by setting aside Salic Law favoring male heirs by father Ferdinand.
1 CONT Her mother acted as regent, while her uncle Don Carlos and his Carlist
1 CONT supporters were "defeated with great violence" claiming the throne in 1839.
1 CONT Declared of age in 1843, her reign was troubled by intrigues, civil strife,
1 CONT and great political instability. Deposed in the Revolution of 1868, she
1 CONT escaped to France, abdicationg in 1870 in favor of her son, Alfonso XII,
1 CONT finally succeeding to the throne in 1875.
0 @N6000000002188434648@ NOTE Queen Isabella II, Queen of Spain From 1833 to 1868, was the daughter of King Ferdinand VII and of Maria Christina. Her uncle, Prince Carlos, contested her succession under the Salic law, and thus the Carlist Wars began. Isabella 
1 CONC was under the regency of her mother until 1840, when Espartero seized power. After his regency (1841-1843) was overthrown, Isabella was declared of age. In 1846 the queen married her cousin, Francisco de Asís, and her sister, Luis
1 CONC a Fernanda, married a son of Louis Philippe of France, the Duke of Montpensier. These Spanish marriages, which contravened earlier Anglo-French agreements about the choice of husbands for the two sisters, aroused the anger of Engl
1 CONC and, who feared a Franco-Spanish rapprochement, and caused a temporary severance of the entente between England and France. Isabella's rule was one of party conflicts among moderates, progressives, and liberal unionists and of con
1 CONC tinuous cabinet changes. Narváez, Espartero, and O'Donnell were among her premiers. Frequent rebellions culminated in 1868 in the insurrection led by Serrano and Juan Prim, and Isabella was deposed. She spent the rest of her life 
1 CONC in France. In 1870 she abdicated her rights in favor of her son, Alfonso XII.
